course: books
lesson: 62
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter X, part 2: The Further Adventures of Toad (into the canal, the stolen horse and «a shilling a leg»)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Laughed at, Toad loses his temper completely. He shouts that she is a «common, low, fat barge-woman» and that he is a well-known, distinguished Toad. She peers under his bonnet — a Toad! — grabs him by a front leg and a back leg, and flings him spinning through the air into the canal.
explain: Toad swims to the bank, the wet cotton gown dragging at him, and runs after the barge wanting revenge. She calls out a last joke about ironing his face. So Toad unties her horse from the tow-rope, jumps on its back and gallops away across the fields, while the barge runs aground and the woman shouts «Stop, stop, stop!» — «I've heard that song before,» laughs Toad.
explain: Miles later, on a wide common, he finds a man by a caravan with a pot of stew bubbling over a fire. The smell makes Toad truly hungry for the first time. «Want to sell that there horse of yours?» asks the man.
explain: Toad pretends the horse is a prize animal he could never sell — then asks how much. «Shillin' a leg,» says the man: four shillings. He raises it to five. Toad bargains for six shillings and sixpence and as much stew as he can eat, and gets it. Full, dry and with money in his pocket again, Toad sets off in the best of spirits — though the horse he sold was never his.
fact: Toad shouts: «You common, low, fat barge-woman! ... I will not be laughed at by a bargewoman!» — rude, snobbish words; calling someone «common» or «fat» to hurt them is unkind.
fact: She peers under his bonnet: «Why, so you are! ... A horrid, nasty, crawly Toad! And in my nice clean barge, too!»
fact: She grabs him by a fore-leg and a hind-leg and throws him; «the world turned suddenly upside down» and he lands in the canal with a loud splash, with duck-weed in his eyes.
fact: The barge-woman's last joke: «Put yourself through your mangle, washerwoman, and iron your face and crimp it, and you'll pass for quite a decent-looking Toad!» (a mangle = a machine with rollers for squeezing water out of wet washing; to crimp = to press into little waves)
fact: «Solid revenge was what he wanted, not cheap, windy, verbal triumphs.» He unties the tow-rope, jumps on the horse and gallops off; that is stealing the horse.
fact: The barge runs aground (gets stuck on the bottom) on the other side of the canal; the woman shouts «Stop, stop, stop!», just as the policemen on the engine shouted in chapter VIII.
fact: The horse's gallop soon became a trot, and the trot a steady walk; at last it stopped on a common to nibble the grass.
fact: On a common (open wild land with gorse and bramble) he finds a caravan and a man sitting on an upturned bucket, smoking, with an iron pot of stew on a fire of sticks.
fact: The book calls the man a «gipsy» and says gipsies were «very fond of horse-dealing». That is an old idea about a whole people; today we know it is wrong to judge anyone by the group they come from. Gypsy and Traveller people are just people, each different.
fact: Selling the horse had not occurred to Toad before. At first he said he couldn't: who would take the washing home to his customers? He loved the horse too much, he said; the man answered «Try and love a donkey. Some people do.»
fact: Toad's sales talk: the horse is «a blood horse, he is, partly; not the part you see, of course—another part», and once «a Prize Hackney» (a blood horse = a well-bred horse; a hackney = a riding or carriage horse).
fact: «Shillin' a leg.» A horse has four legs, so that is four shillings; the man then offers five shillings, saying that is «three-and-sixpence more than the animal's worth».
fact: Old money: 12 pence made a shilling; «three-and-sixpence» = 3 shillings and 6 pence.
fact: Toad's last word: six shillings and sixpence, cash, plus «as much breakfast as I can possibly eat, at one sitting», and the harness thrown in. He gets it.
fact: The stew is made of partridges, pheasants, chickens, hares, rabbits, pea-hens, guinea-fowls «and one or two other things»; Toad «stuffed, and stuffed, and stuffed».
fact: The man «knew the riverside well» and tells Toad which way to go; Toad leaves «big, and strong, and careless, and self-confident».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «tamping» (Wales) = furious; «proper job» (West Country) = well done; «bussin'» = really tasty; «mid» = just average; «servo» (Australia) = petrol station.
exercise: What did Toad shout at the barge-woman when she laughed? | «You common, low, fat barge-woman!» | «Thank you kindly, ma'am, for the lift» | «I am a poor unhappy washerwoman» | «Stop, stop, stop!»
exercise: How did the barge-woman find out he was a Toad? | She peered closely under his bonnet | He took off his bonnet to show her his face, proudly | A policeman told her | She saw his crinkly paws
exercise: «A horrid, nasty, crawly Toad! And in my nice clean ... » what? | Barge | Kitchen | Canal, where my horse walks every day | Wash-tub
exercise: How did she get rid of him? | She grabbed a fore-leg and a hind-leg and flung him into the canal | She pushed him off with the long pole she used for steering in the shallows | She called the police | She tipped him into the wash-tub
exercise: What made swimming to the bank hard for Toad? | The wet cotton gown | A strong current | A heavy bag of money | The horse's rope wrapped tightly round his legs
exercise: What did Toad have in his eyes when he came up? | Duck-weed | Soap bubbles | Tears of sorrow, because he missed his friends so much | Coal-dust from the barge
exercise: «Put yourself through your mangle... and iron your face.» What is a mangle? | A machine with rollers for squeezing water out of washing | A big kettle | A heavy iron for pressing the creases out of shirts and sheets | A kind of boat that carries washing along canals
exercise: What did Toad want instead of «cheap, windy, verbal triumphs»? | Solid revenge | A proper apology | Money for his ruined dress | His breakfast back
exercise: What did Toad do to the barge-horse? | Untied it, jumped on its back and galloped off | Sold it to the barge-woman's husband at the next lock | Fed it | Hid it in a wood
exercise: What happened to the barge after the horse was gone? | It ran aground on the other side of the canal | It sank | It floated away gently down the canal towards the river | It caught fire
exercise: «Stop, stop, stop!» shouted the barge-woman. What did Toad answer? | «I've heard that song before» | «Never!» | «Poop-poop!» | «Toad's luck again, I always come out on top!»
exercise: How fast did the barge-horse keep going? | Its gallop soon became a trot and then a walk | It galloped all day without stopping once, all the way home | It stopped at once | It ran backwards
exercise: Where did the horse stop to nibble grass? | On a wide common with gorse and bramble | In the Wild Wood, deep among the stoats' and weasels' holes | By the Red Lion | In the paddock at Toad Hall
exercise: What was the man by the caravan sitting on? | A bucket turned upside down | A log | The steps of his caravan, painted canary-yellow | A saddle
exercise: What hung over the fire? | An iron pot of stew | A kettle on the boil | A frying-pan full of sizzling bacon and eggs | A bird-cage with a canary
exercise: «Want to sell that there horse of yours?» Why did this surprise Toad? | It had not occurred to him to turn the horse into money | He loved the horse | He thought nobody would ever want to buy such a tired old barge-horse | He did not understand
exercise: Who, said Toad, would take the washing home to his customers if he sold the horse? | Nobody — that was his excuse for not selling | His married daughter, who lived near Toad Hall | His twenty girls | The barge-woman's husband
exercise: «Try and love a donkey. Some people do.» Who said this? | The man by the caravan | Toad | The barge-woman, laughing from her boat | The horse
exercise: «A blood horse, he is, partly; not the part you see.» Why is this funny? | It is nonsense — you can't be well-bred in a part nobody can see | It is true | The horse is half donkey, and the donkey half is the half you can see | Toad cannot see well without his spectacles
exercise: How much was «a shilling a leg»? | Four shillings | Only one shilling | Six shillings and sixpence | Five shillings exactly
exercise: What did the man offer next? | Five shillings | Four and a half | A donkey | Ten shillings and a fine new bonnet
exercise: What did Toad finally get for the horse? | Six shillings and sixpence and as much stew as he could eat | Five shillings and a ride home in the caravan | A pound | Four shillings
exercise: Which of these was NOT in the stew? | Fish | Pheasants | Hares | Guinea-fowls
exercise: Who told Toad which way to go afterwards? | The man by the caravan, who knew the riverside well | The horse | The barge-woman's husband, on his way back from catching rabbits | A rabbit
exercise: How did Toad feel when he set off again? | Big, strong, careless and self-confident | Ashamed and sorry for stealing the barge-woman's horse | Tired and frightened | Sick from too much stew
test: Ellie, proper tamping (furious): «that barge-woman was well out of order chucking him in the canal!» What started it? | Toad lost his temper and called her rude names first | She found out he had stolen her purse from the cabin while she was steering | Toad tipped her washing into the canal | Her husband told her to
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad sold his own horse to the gipsy.» | False: the horse belonged to the barge-woman — Toad had stolen it | True: it was his own horse, brought all the way from the stables at Toad Hall | True: the gaoler's daughter gave it to him | False: he gave it away free
test: Why does Toad say «I've heard that song before»? | Everyone chasing him keeps shouting «Stop, stop, stop!», like the men on the engine | He has heard her sing earlier | It is the Ducks' Ditty | It is the first line of his own conceited song about himself
test: Jordan: «gipsy food was mid (just average) innit, Toad hardly ate any.» Put it right. | It was the most beautiful stew in the world; Toad stuffed himself | Right, he ate one spoonful and said it was too salty | He ate toast instead | He gave his to the horse
test: «Gipsies were very fond of horse-dealing.» What should a reader today think of this line? | It is an old idea about a whole people; we should not judge anyone by their group | It is a true fact about every Gypsy person | It is a joke the man by the caravan told about himself and his family | It means gipsies hate horses
test: What would have happened if Toad had walked away without bargaining? | He would have stayed hungry and penniless, with a stolen horse | The kind man would have given him the stew anyway, and some money too | The horse would have followed the barge home | The police would have given him a lift
test: Why was any money «all clear profit» to Toad? | The horse had cost him nothing — he had taken it | He had bought it very cheaply from the barge-woman's husband at the lock | He was rich | The gipsy paid twice
test: Ruby, «proper job!» (well done, West Country): «Toad was dead honest in that deal.» Is she right? | No: he sold a stolen horse and told lies about it | Yes: every word he said was true | Yes: he even sent the barge-woman the money for her horse at once | No: he never sold the horse
test: Looking back: in chapter II, the old grey horse pulled Toad's caravan «without having been consulted». What happened to that caravan? | A motor-car frightened the horse and the caravan ended in a ditch, a wreck | It was sold to a gipsy | It was stolen by weasels and hidden in the Wild Wood until winter | It sank in the river
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, what food first began to cheer Toad up in prison? | Bubble-and-squeak, then hot buttered toast and tea | A gipsy stew | Cold chicken and ginger-beer from the Rat's luncheon-basket | Sardines and biscuits
test: Looking back: in chapter I, who fell into the river when a boat tipped over, and who pulled him out? | Mole fell in; the Rat pulled him out | Toad fell in; the barge-woman pulled him out | Rat fell in; Mole pulled him out | Otter fell in; Badger pulled him out
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, the magistrates gave Toad twenty years. Which of his offences did the Clerk say deserved the most years? | Cheeking the police | Stealing the motor-car | Driving to the public danger | Escaping from the inn
test: Looking back: after escaping in chapter VI, Toad called his trick «brain against brute force». How does this part show the same Toad? | He again thinks his cleverness wins, even when it is really stealing and lying | He now admits he was wrong and goes home | He stops, thinks hard, and decides to give the horse back to its owner | He refuses to bargain
test: Which word sums up Toad's mood at the END of this part? | Conceited | Truly sorry | Frightened | Very lonely
test: ★★★ The man says five shillings is «three-and-sixpence more than the animal's worth». So what does he say the horse is worth? | One shilling and sixpence | Three shillings and sixpence | Eight shillings and sixpence | Two shillings
test: ★★★ Toad got six shillings and sixpence. How much MORE is that than the first offer of «a shilling a leg»? | Two shillings and sixpence | One shilling and sixpence | Six shillings and sixpence exactly | Sixpence
test: ★★★ With 12 pence in a shilling, how many pence is six shillings and sixpence? | 78 | 66 | 72 | 65
exam: Identify the means by which the barge-woman ascertained Toad's true identity. | By peering closely beneath his bonnet | By the testimony of her husband | By reading of his escape in a newspaper | By the inexpertness of his steering
exam: Ascertain the course of action adopted by Toad in pursuit of «solid revenge». | He detached the barge-horse from the tow-rope and departed upon it | He capsized the barge, with its cargo and the barge-woman's washing, in the canal | He laid a complaint before the magistrates | He sang a derisive song from the bank
exam: It is given that the first offer was «a shilling a leg» for a horse. Compute the sum thereby offered. | Four shillings | One shilling only | Six shillings and sixpence | Five shillings precisely
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the terms upon which the transaction was concluded. | Six shillings and sixpence in cash, together with as much breakfast as Toad could eat at one sitting | Five shillings, payable upon delivery at Toad Hall | An exchange of the horse for a donkey | Four shillings and the gipsy's caravan
